      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The rule fired on same-file co-occurrence of a network primitive and a suspected execution sink. In the supplied source, the only network activity is a same-origin GET and POST against the local SillyTavern server's per-user file storage endpoints, authenticated with the host's standard request headers, to load and save the extension's own per-character facts document. The retrieved JSON is parsed, validated, and used solely as text for prompt injection and persistence. The constructs that plausibly matched the execution-sink heuristic are a promise-based timer delay that receives a function reference rather than a string, and a byte-wise string encoding helper used only to base64-encode the saved facts payload for the host upload API. No retrieved or remotely sourced data reaches any string-evaluated execution mechanism, there is no external destination, the stored file name is sanitized against path characters, and the persistence and prompt-injection capabilities match the documented purpose of persisting user facts per character.",
      "layman_explanation": "The scanner noticed that this extension both makes network requests and contains something that looked like it could run code, and flagged that combination. Reading the actual code, the extension only downloads and saves its own small notes file on the user's own SillyTavern server, and those notes are used purely as text in chats. Nothing that is downloaded is ever run as code, and nothing is sent anywhere outside the user's own server. This is exactly what the extension says it does, so the warning is a false alarm.",
